WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.389
Now: adaptive thinking and effort levels.

2
00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:07.171
Current Claude models produce thinking content blocks before their visible answer.

3
00:00:07.451 --> 00:00:17.243
In adaptive mode, which Anthropic recommends for current models, the model decides when to think and how much, based on the request’s difficulty and on the effort setting.

4
00:00:17.523 --> 00:00:19.379
Effort, set as output_config.

5
00:00:19.659 --> 00:00:26.507
effort, ranges from low through medium, high, and xhigh to max (not every model supports every level).

6
00:00:26.787 --> 00:00:37.325
It is a behavioral signal rather than a strict budget, and it applies to the whole response, not only the thinking: lower effort also means terser text and fewer tool calls.

7
00:00:37.605 --> 00:00:41.723
If you need a hard ceiling on spending, max_tokens is the strict limit.

8
00:00:42.003 --> 00:00:44.947
Defaults differ by model; on Claude Opus 5.

9
00:00:45.227 --> 00:00:52.843
5, thinking is always on and the default effort is medium, so set effort explicitly rather than relying on the default.

10
00:00:53.123 --> 00:00:55.832
Two details matter when you write code against this.

11
00:00:56.112 --> 00:01:07.077
First, the text in a thinking block is a summary of the reasoning, not the raw chain of thought, and on many current models it is omitted (an empty string) unless you ask for summaries.

12
00:01:07.357 --> 00:01:13.117
Second, thinking blocks arrive before the text, so select blocks by type, never by position.

13
00:01:13.397 --> 00:01:17.600
Other providers have converged on similar controls, under different names.

14
00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:33.773
At the time of writing, OpenAI’s reasoning models take a reasoning setting with an effort level, Google’s Gemini models take a thinking_level, and xAI’s Grok reasoning models take reasoning_effort (defaulting to high, with reasoning that cannot be turned off).

15
00:01:34.053 --> 00:01:41.157
Check each provider’s current docs before relying on specific levels or defaults; they change with model releases.

16
00:01:41.437 --> 00:01:43.080
What does this mean for prompting?

17
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:55.691
Anthropic’s guidance is that general instructions such as “think thoroughly” often work better than a hand-written step-by-step plan, because the model’s own reasoning frequently beats the plan you would prescribe.

18
00:01:55.971 --> 00:02:07.363
Manual chain-of-thought prompting is now a fallback for when thinking is off; on models with thinking always on, the docs say to rely on thinking, at a lower effort level if cost matters.

19
00:02:07.643 --> 00:02:20.315
OpenAI’s guide offers a memorable analogy: a reasoning model is like a senior colleague you can give a goal to, while a non-reasoning model is like a junior one who needs explicit instructions.
